<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Seljuk Sultanate of Kerman</h1><p class="st-meta">1041 CE – 1186 CE (approx.) · capital Bardasir</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A branch of the Seljuk dynasty that ruled Kerman in south-eastern Iran for nearly 150 years and extended its power across the sea to Oman.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qavurt">Qavurt</a> (Qara Arslan), elder brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a>, took the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in the 1040s as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> expanded across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He conquered the mountain tribes of the region and crossed the Gulf to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> tributary. He later rebelled against his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik-Shah</a> and was executed in 1074, but his sons kept Kerman.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> ruled from Bardasir (Kerman city) with Turkish troops and Persian officials. Arslan Shah I (1101 to 1142) presided over a period of prosperity based on trade routes from the Gulf ports of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Hormuz</a> and Tiz to Khurasan, and the rulers built mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>. The dynasty was largely independent of the main Seljuk line.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a> Shah's death in 1170, his sons fought a long civil war, inviting outside intervention. In 1186 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a> under Malik <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinar">Dinar</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>, ending the dynasty.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1041 CE – 1186 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.42%;width:2.89%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1041 CE – 1186 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bardasir</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qavurt, Arslan Shah I, Tughril Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/oman">Oman</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kerman Seljuks, Seljuks of Kirman</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1114&amp;focus=seljuk-sultanate-of-kerman" aria-label="Seljuk Sultanate of Kerman on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6150 1085 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Seljuk Sultanate of Kerman lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6470 1341L6481 1127"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6481 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6470 1341h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6470 1341h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bardasir. <a href="/atlas?year=1114&amp;focus=seljuk-sultanate-of-kerman">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
